<#--
  Message metrics fixture.

  Wiring contract, and the reason this header exists: a message document's "request-id" is the id of
  the *connection* that carried it, and its "correlation-id" is the message's own id. The two-phase
  message join reads the first — it collects request-ids from v4-metrics.ftl and narrows the message
  query with terms:{request-id:[...]} — so every "request-id" below must also appear as a
  "request-id" in v4-metrics.ftl, for the same api-id, or the join silently matches nothing.

  That is what had gone wrong: connection ids had been written into "correlation-id" instead, leaving
  only 2 of 9 message request-ids joinable. Any query then returned near-nothing on the joined path,
  which made the joined and the unjoined path indistinguishable in test and hid whichever was broken.

  Every connection referenced below also carries an "entrypoint-id", deliberately: a test forces the
  join back on by adding an ENTRYPOINT condition, and a connection without that field can be matched
  by no ENTRYPOINT value at all — its messages would vanish from the joined path for a reason that
  has nothing to do with what is being tested.
-->
